\title{An Integer Model of Anti-Gravity Theorems:\\
Logarithmic Proof Cost and the Support Trade-Off}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-19}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce and rigorously analyze a number-theoretic model of \emph{anti-gravity
theorems} --- results that carry high structural load (many dependents) yet require
little to prove. In a fixed universe of size $N \in \mathbb{N}$, we model a result as a
positive integer $d$ and assign to it two quantities: its \emph{support}
$\supp(N,d) = \floorr{N/d}$, measuring how much of the universe rests upon it, and its
\emph{proof cost} $\proofcost(d) = \Om(d)$, the number of prime factors of $d$ counted
with multiplicity, measuring how many irreducible steps build it. Our central structural
result is the \emph{logarithmic cost bound} $2^{\Om(d)} \le d$ for every $d > 0$,
equivalently $\Om(d) \le \log_2 d$, derived from the single fact that every prime is at
least $2$. Combining this with denominator antitonicity of integer division yields the
\emph{anti-gravity support trade-off} $\supp(N,d) \le \floorr{N/2^{\Om(d)}}$: a result's
reach is bounded by the universe size divided by two raised to its proof cost, so each
unit of proof cost at most halves the support ceiling. We characterize the extremal
objects --- the unit $1$ (zero cost, full support $N$), the primes (unit cost, support up
to $\floorr{N/2}$), and the powers of two (maximal cost for their size) --- and discuss
why anti-gravity results are abundant. All results are stated with full mathematical
content and proof sketches, and have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring observation about mathematics is that its most structurally important results
are often among its simplest: the Pythagorean theorem, unique factorization, the extreme
value theorem, and countless basic lemmas underpin enormous superstructures while
admitting short statements and short proofs. We call such a result an \emph{anti-gravity
theorem}: it supports much, yet resists the ``downward pull'' of proof complexity. In
physical structures load-bearing and weight travel together; in mathematics they come
apart, and the load-bearing members are often the lightest.

This paper makes the metaphor exact via a fully precise model in which both ``how much a
result supports'' and ``how much it costs to prove'' are concrete natural numbers, and in
which the anti-gravity trade-off between them is a theorem. The model lives in elementary
number theory: results are positive integers, support is integer division, and proof cost
is the count of prime factors with multiplicity.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A precise model (Section~\ref{sec:model}) of \emph{support} and \emph{proof cost}.
  \item The \textbf{logarithmic cost bound} $2^{\Om(d)} \le d$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:log}),
        via a combinatorial product lemma (Lemma~\ref{lem:prod}).
  \item The \textbf{anti-gravity support trade-off}
        $\supp(N,d) \le \floorr{N/2^{\Om(d)}}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff}), via
        denominator antitonicity (Lemma~\ref{lem:anti}).
  \item A characterization of extremal objects and a discussion of abundance
        (Section~\ref{sec:extremal}).
\end{itemize}

\section{The model: support and proof cost}\label{sec:model}

Throughout, $N, d, k$ denote natural numbers and $\floorr{\cdot/\cdot}$ denotes truncated
(floor) integer division.

\begin{definition}[Support]
Fix a universe size $N \in \mathbb{N}$. The \emph{support} of a positive integer $d$ in
$N$ is
\[ \supp(N,d) := \floorr{N/d}. \]
\end{definition}

\noindent Interpretation: $\supp(N,d)$ counts how many multiples of $d$ lie at or below
$N$, i.e.\ how many slots of the universe rest on $d$. Small $d$ yields large support;
large $d$ yields small support. This is the model's \emph{gravitational weight}.

\begin{fact}[\texttt{support\_eq\_div}]
$\supp(N,d) = \floorr{N/d}$ for all $N, d$.
\end{fact}

\begin{definition}[Proof cost]
The \emph{proof cost} of $d \in \mathbb{N}$ is
\[ \proofcost(d) := \Om(d) = \text{length of the list of prime factors of } d
   \text{ (with multiplicity).} \]
\end{definition}

\noindent If $d = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_m$ is the prime factorization with repetition, then
$\proofcost(d) = m$. Examples: $\proofcost(1) = 0$, $\proofcost(p) = 1$ for prime $p$,
$\proofcost(12) = 3$, $\proofcost(2^k) = k$. Each prime factor is an irreducible
justification step; $\Om(d)$ is the length of the shortest multiplicative derivation of
$d$ from the primes.

\begin{definition}[Anti-gravity result]
Relative to a universe $N$, a result $d$ is \emph{anti-gravity to degree $(w,c)$} if
$\supp(N,d) \ge w$ and $\proofcost(d) \le c$; informally, large support and small proof
cost.
\end{definition}

\section{The logarithmic cost bound}

The keystone is that proof cost cannot exceed the base-two logarithm of magnitude.

\begin{lemma}[Product bound; \texttt{two\_pow\_length\_le\_prod\_of\_forall\_two\_le}]
\label{lem:prod}
Let $l$ be a finite list of natural numbers with $x \ge 2$ for every entry $x \in l$.
Then $2^{|l|} \le \prod_{x \in l} x$, where $|l|$ is the length of $l$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $l$. For the empty list both sides equal $1$. For $l = x :: xs$ with
$x \ge 2$ and all entries of $xs$ at least $2$, the induction hypothesis gives
$2^{|xs|} \le \prod xs$, so
$2^{|l|} = 2^{|xs|}\cdot 2 \le (\prod xs)\cdot x = \prod l$,
using $2 \le x$ and monotonicity of multiplication on $\mathbb{N}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic cost bound; \texttt{two\_pow\_proofCost\_le}]\label{thm:log}
For every $d > 0$, $\; 2^{\proofcost(d)} \le d$, equivalently
$\proofcost(d) = \Om(d) \le \log_2 d$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $l$ be the list of prime factors of $d$. Every entry is a prime, hence $\ge 2$, so
Lemma~\ref{lem:prod} gives $2^{|l|} \le \prod l$. For $d > 0$ the fundamental theorem of
arithmetic gives $\prod l = d$, while $|l| = \proofcost(d)$. Substituting yields
$2^{\proofcost(d)} \le d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The bound is tight: for $d = 2^k$, $\proofcost(d) = k$ and $2^{\proofcost(d)} = d$.
Powers of two maximize proof cost for their magnitude.
\end{remark}

\section{The anti-gravity support trade-off}

\begin{lemma}[Denominator antitonicity; \texttt{div\_le\_div\_of\_le\_right}]\label{lem:anti}
For all $N$ and all $a,b$ with $0 < a \le b$, $\; \floorr{N/b} \le \floorr{N/a}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the adjunction of truncated division, $\floorr{N/b} \le \floorr{N/a}$ iff
$\floorr{N/b}\cdot a \le N$. Since $a \le b$,
$\floorr{N/b}\cdot a \le \floorr{N/b}\cdot b \le N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Anti-gravity support trade-off; \texttt{support\_le\_div\_two\_pow}]
\label{thm:tradeoff}
For every $d > 0$ and every universe size $N$,
\[ \supp(N,d) \le \floorr{N / 2^{\proofcost(d)}}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\supp(N,d) = \floorr{N/d}$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:anti} with
$a = 2^{\proofcost(d)}$ and $b = d$: $0 < a$ since powers of two are positive, and
$a \le b$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:log}. Hence
$\floorr{N/d} \le \floorr{N/2^{\proofcost(d)}}$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Interpretation.} The right-hand side decreases weakly as
$\proofcost(d)$ increases: each unit of proof cost at most halves the support ceiling. A
result costing $c$ steps supports at most $\floorr{N/2^c}$ of the universe. Expensive
results are structurally peripheral; only cheap results can be load-bearing.

\begin{corollary}[Reach forces cheapness]
If $\supp(N,d) > \floorr{N/2^c}$ for some $c$, then $\proofcost(d) < c$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff}: if $\proofcost(d) \ge c$ then
$2^{\proofcost(d)} \ge 2^c$, so $\floorr{N/2^{\proofcost(d)}} \le \floorr{N/2^c}$ and
$\supp(N,d) \le \floorr{N/2^c}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Extremal objects and abundance}\label{sec:extremal}

\begin{proposition}[The unit is maximally anti-gravity]
$\proofcost(1) = 0$ and $\supp(N,1) = N$; the trade-off bound
$\supp(N,1) \le \floorr{N/2^0} = N$ holds with equality.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Primes are the cheapest nontrivial load-bearers]
For prime $p$, $\proofcost(p) = 1$ and $\supp(N,p) \le \floorr{N/2}$. A prime can support
up to half the universe on one proof step; support exceeding $\floorr{N/2}$ forces proof
cost $0$, i.e.\ $d = 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Powers of two are maximally heavy for their size]
For $d = 2^k$, $\proofcost(d) = k$ and $2^{\proofcost(d)} = d$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:log}
holds with equality and $\supp(N,2^k) = \floorr{N/2^k}$.
\end{proposition}

\paragraph{Abundance.} Since $\proofcost(d) \le \log_2 d$, proof cost grows only
logarithmically in magnitude: doubling $d$ raises its maximum possible cost by at most
$1$. The small integers --- those with the largest support --- overwhelmingly have small
proof cost, so low cost and high support both favor small $d$. The two desiderata of an
anti-gravity result align, and anti-gravity results are plentiful.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Proof cost via trial division.} Compute $\Om(d)$ by repeatedly dividing
        out the smallest prime factor; $O(\sqrt{d})$ per factor, with
        $\Om(d) \le \log_2 d$ factors.
  \item \textbf{Anti-gravity certificate.} Given $N, d>0, c$, verify
        $\supp(N,d) \le \floorr{N/2^c}$ and $\proofcost(d) \le c$; by
        Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff} the first is automatic once $c \ge \proofcost(d)$.
  \item \textbf{Extremal enumeration.} For a budget $c$, list $d$ with $\Om(d) \le c$ in
        increasing order; the smallest maximize $\supp(N,d)$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications}

Library prioritization (which lemmas can be both cheap and load-bearing), curriculum
design (teach foundational, elementary results first), and complexity intuition (magnitude
buys proof steps only logarithmically).

\section{Discussion and future work}

The integer model is deliberately minimal, capturing the cost/support trade-off with the
fewest moving parts and a sharp inequality (Theorem~\ref{thm:tradeoff}). Its extremal
objects line up with intuition about foundational versus peripheral results. Natural
extensions are: weighted dependencies, transitive load (the reachable dependency cone),
algorithmic extraction from real formal-library import graphs, and extremal bounds for
dependency-dense anti-gravity sets.

